e city  we now go to suzanne webb  the home secretary pretty patel there  who's given a statement to the house of  commons  announcing an independent review to be  carried out into the met police's  handling of  the vigil at clapham common on saturday  night following  the death of sarah everard  she reiterated the phrase that so many  have  uh said since the death of sarah everard  she was just  walking home and said that since  reopening their consultation on violence  against women and girls on friday  there have been 78 000 responses  that is up from 18 000 over the 10-week  period  of the initial consultation such is the  strength of feeling  and the reaction to the death of sarah  everard  let's bring in our correspondent chief  political correspondent john craig who  was listening in  to the exchanges many questions about  the  metropolitan police handling of this  also about the upcoming debate over the  police crime sentencing  and courts bill and some on the tory  back benches concerned about  coronavirus regulations and the part  they may have played john  yes they were the lock down skeptics the  sort of people you'd expect to  say those things there was sir charles  walker uh  saying that we passed these uh laws uh  criminalizing uh public  protest uh there were complaints  actually on  from those mps there was philip davis  making some suggestions about  more closed circuit tv to protect women  and so on  uh sir graham brady however on the labor  side  in particular there was strong criticism  of the police action from the likes of  diane abbott and harriet harmon there  was only it was only ed  davey the liberal democrat leader who  directly called for the resignation of  uh cressida dick the met commissioner  of course she should resign he said he  was immediately slapped down by ian  duncan smith  former conservative leader who said that  he'd been contacted by a female officer  who had  been been manhandled he said  and uh and uh threatened during 